{"The goroutine measures Legacy.DeleteCollection but records it with the storage metric helper. Legacy delete-collection latency and errors will be reported as unified-storage work, so mode 3 can hide failures in the async legacy cleanup path": 0.9951582551002502, "Successful deletes record the object name in the metric label where the rest of mode 3 records the resource kind. Dashboards grouped by kind will miss these deletes, and object names can create high-cardinality metric series": 0.9662204384803772, "The async legacy write derives its timeout from the request context after the unified write has already succeeded. If the client disconnects or the request context is canceled before the goroutine finishes, the legacy create sees the canceled child context and can be skipped after the API has returned success, leaving mode 3 with no legacy safety copy": 0.9774347543716431, "The branch now returns on any unified-store delete error including NotFound, so mode 3 no longer attempts the legacy delete when unified storage is already missing the object. In a partial migration state where the object still exists only in legacy, delete returns NotFound and leaves the legacy copy behind": 0.9758850336074829, "The legacy update now reuses the original objInfo after unified storage has already persisted its result. If Legacy.Update calls UpdatedObject again against a different old object, resourceVersion, or request-backed state, the legacy write can store a different object or fail validation after the API has already returned the unified result": 0.890914261341095}